  "query_preview": "..Today...Warm conditions and mainly dry conditions \nare expected for today as a trough moves through to the north. The\nmain story for this afternoon is gusty southwest winds and dry \nconditions, leading to areas of critical fire weather conditions \nacross portions of northern Arizona. Peak gusts this afternoon \nlook to largely be within the 40-45 mph range, especially over \nwestern Arizona, with some potentially higher gusts downwind of \nterrain features.\n\nWith some mid-level moisture in place, a few showers and possibly a \nthunderstorm will be possible mainly north of the Mogollon Rim. \nHowever, with a very dry sub-cloud layer, it will be difficult for \nany precipitation to actually reach the surface. Thus, much of the \nactivity will be virga outside of possible a couple of sprinkles \nover the higher terrain of Navajo Nation. Brief periods of gusty, \nerratic winds will be possible around any areas of virga this \nafternoon.\n\nThursday and Friday...In the wake of the trough, zonal flow looks to \nset in for the end of the work week. Continued dry conditions and \nlighter winds return as a result. Temperatures look to remain about \n5-10F above normal through the period.\n\nSaturday through Tuesday...A trough looks to move through the Inner \nMountain West this weekend. As a result, gustier winds and cooler \ntemperatures look to return to northern Arizona. Gusts upwards of 45 \nmph look probable by Sunday as winds aloft look to peak. Given that \ndry air is still in place, additional fire weather concerns are \nexpected over the weekend.\n\nMoisture remains fairly sparse with the trough, thus precipitation \nchances look slim to none through the period. EPS ensembles do show \na slight chance, maybe around Monday or Tuesday as some mid-level \nmoisture increases however the GEFS looks less optimistic on this \npotential. For now, chances will remain below mentionable, \nespecially given the still very dry surface layer.",
